module nupp.mem

--[[
C storage, and the checked views that make a raw pointer safe to index.

Nothing is re-exported here: every member is a module of its own, reached by the
name it has.

```nupp
const span = require("nupp.mem.span")

local struct Point
    x: number
    y: number
end

const storage = carray(Point, 4)
const points = span.fromCarray(storage, 4)

print(points[1].x)
```

- [](nupp.mem.span) -- borrowed views over storage something else roots.
- [](nupp.mem.array) -- owned contiguous arrays, native and Wasm.
- [](nupp.mem.heap) -- owned malloc-backed arrays.
- [](nupp.mem.soa) -- structure-of-arrays storage.
- [](nupp.mem.indexed) -- the one inclusive range they all validate against.
- [](nupp.mem.arena) -- leases zero-filled struct rows from pages that never
  move, so it hands storage out and takes it back rather than viewing it.

A view knows its element count, and every index and slice is checked against it
before the only unsafe pointer arithmetic in the standard library. Record
instances backed by a Lua table are [](nupp.util.Pool) instead, which needs no C
storage and so lives with the general-purpose types.

See [c-interop.md](docs/learn/runtime/c-interop/index.md) for what crossing the
C boundary costs, and
[ownership.md](docs/learn/runtime/ownership/borrowing.md) for the borrow rules
these views are written against.
]]
